WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.321 --> 00:00:08.007
[Music starts] [Audio Description - not captioned]

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:11.760
[L. Aravind Iyer, PhD] Early in my&nbsp;
life, I wanted to be a paleontologist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.213
and that's what actually&nbsp;
led me to molecular biology.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.788
At one level, I could say that I wish to&nbsp;
understand the whole protein universe.

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:35.520
Proteins can be divided into evolutionary units&nbsp;
- is a part of a protein which is preserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.920
over evolution because natural selection&nbsp;
is maintaining that part for some reason.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.280
And one realization which dawned on us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:51.760
starting around the early 90s, and this was a&nbsp;
very profound realization for all of biology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.240
is that there is a relatively small number of&nbsp;
these evolutionary units of proteins, which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:06.560
term domains, which constitutes the entire protein&nbsp;
universe of all organisms across the tree of life.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.560
If we can understand the functions of these units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.575
then that goes a long way towards&nbsp;
understanding what organisms do.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.388
And given that there are many gaps in&nbsp;
our understanding of what organisms do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.360
one way to get at it is to&nbsp;
first find all these domains.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.880
The second aspect of it is&nbsp;
predicting functions for them.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.840
The first phase of my research, we&nbsp;
captured most of the low hanging fruit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.720
which were the big families&nbsp;
conserved across all organisms.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.760
Now we are moving on to&nbsp;
the more difficult terrain.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.440
But the difficult terrain&nbsp;
also holds a lot of promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.040
because many un-understood functions are&nbsp;
hiding within that difficult terrain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.394
it gives these offshoots in the&nbsp;
form of biotechnological reagents.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.720
There are things like restriction&nbsp;
enzymes, the CRISPR systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.957
and DNA modification systems. All of&nbsp;
these have become very popular reagents.

00:02:11.411 --> 00:02:18.880
NLM is a world leader in the analysis of&nbsp;
protein sequences, protein structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.720
and inferring evolution from&nbsp;
these bits of information.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.751
And this has been a very long-standing&nbsp;
interest of mine so this is the place to be.

00:02:45.855 --> 00:02:47.218
[Music fades to silence]

